{# The citation protocol of every run that has `cite_documents`.

   `citation_artefact` names what the model writes, for example an answer or a report.
   `citation_resolver` says who turns a handle back into a document. A worker runs on the
   lead's chat session, so its handles are the lead's handles, and its text names the lead.
   The system prompt gives the order of the work. This skill gives the arguments, one
   example for each citation tool, and what to do after a failed entry. The `report` skill
   says which file holds the cited text.
#}
Cited text goes into a workspace file. The `report` skill says how to write and edit it.

{% if has('cite_documents') %}
When your {{ citation_artefact }} relies on {% if web_enabled %}an internal{% else %}a{% endif %} document, call {{ tool('cite_documents') }}.
Give it each document that supports a claim or quote. Use the returned handle beside that claim.
Use a handle beside a claim that relies on an internal document. You can also name a
document to identify it or describe a search result.
One call takes at most 12 citations. Cite what you used, not everything a search returned.
When a search or read returned documents that support a claim, cite those documents.

For each citation give:
- collection and file_hash, copied from a tool result. Never make up a file_hash.
- quote: 5 to 6 consecutive words copied from one line of the document body. Not a header
  line (From, To, Cc, Subject, Date). No span that holds a question mark.
- find: optional. The shortest exact phrase of the quote that the person must see. The card
  opens the document at this phrase.
- why: one line that says what the document supports.

Write the handle that you get back, for example `[D1]`, in your {{ citation_artefact }} where you make the claim.

Example. {{ tool('read_documents') if has('read_documents') else 'A tool' }} returned the email "Re: DRAFT talking points" in the collection enron.
Its body says "The talking points look great to me. Best of luck today." Your {{ citation_artefact }} says
that Jim Steffes approved the draft. Call {{ tool('cite_documents') }} with collection enron, the
file_hash from the result, the quote "talking points look great to me", find "look great",
and why "Steffes approves the draft of 27 December".
Then write "Steffes approved the draft [D1]."
Do not quote "Subject: Re: DRAFT talking points", because it is a header line. Do not quote
"Can you check the notes?", because it holds a question mark.
Line breaks, punctuation and the `>` marks of a quoted e-mail do not need to match.
Missing or absent quotes allocate no handle. Copy 5 to 6 words of the candidate text that the error gives into quote and retry.
Only a returned handle labels a source. Never write your own label in square brackets.
Write the handle as text beside the claim. Do not put it in backticks. `[D1]` in backticks links nothing.
Reuse the returned document handle for additional verified passages. The document has one card.
{% if citation_resolver == "lead" %}
The lead researcher resolves them, so a handle you never allocated is no use to anyone.
{% endif %}
{% endif %}
A handle supports only the claim of its own quote. Do not put a handle beside a claim about
another event, person, date or document. When a citation fails, do not put another handle
in its place. Leave out the claim, or say that the sources read do not confirm it.
When an error gives candidate text, copy it character for character, with its quotation
marks, apostrophes and dashes. When the second call for one passage also fails, stop
quoting that passage.
{% if has('cite_pages') %}
Search for each web source and read its supporting passage with {{ tool('read_page') }}.
Call {{ tool('cite_pages') }} with the read URL and exact source wording in `terms`.

Example. {{ tool('read_page') }} read the URL https://example.org/report. Its text says
"The plant closed in March 2019." Call {{ tool('cite_pages') }} with flat arguments for one page:
url, copied from the read result, terms with the exact phrase, and why.
```json
{"url":"COPY_READ_URL","terms":["The plant closed in March 2019"],"why":"The report gives the closing date."}
```
Then write "The plant closed in March 2019 [W1]." with the handle that the call returned.
Give at most eight terms for one page, each at most 200 characters. Case must match.
One call accepts at most twelve pages in `pages`. Split longer source lists into separate calls.
```json
{"pages":[{"url":"COPY_FIRST_READ_URL","terms":["COPY_EXACT_PHRASE"]},{"url":"COPY_SECOND_READ_URL","terms":["COPY_EXACT_PHRASE"]}]}
```
Copy the returned web handle, for example `[W1]`, beside the claim it supports.
Keep each handle with the URL and quotes from its returned citation object.
An error does not allocate a handle. Omit that source's claims until its citation succeeds.
For absent wording, copy exact text from the returned candidate and call the tool again.
When an error includes `errors[i].retry_arguments`, copy that object unchanged into the next call.
Keep successful citations from a partial result. Retry only the failed pages within the existing retry limit.
For example, this call joins words across a source line break and fails.
```json
{"url":"https://example.org/report","terms":["The plant closed in March 2019"]}
```
The error can return these retry arguments with the exact source text.
```json
{"retry_arguments":{"url":"https://example.org/report","version":"COPY_RETURNED_VERSION","terms":["The plant closed"]}}
```
Call {{ tool('cite_pages') }} with that returned object, including its actual version and exact terms.
```json
{"url":"https://example.org/report","version":"COPY_RETURNED_VERSION","terms":["The plant closed"]}
```
If a call exceeds eight terms, split its terms into groups of at most eight and cite each group separately.
Keep the URL and source version with each group. Changing the call structure does not reduce its term count.
The handle opens the captured page text and its verified quotes.
Use a web citation handle in place of a bare source URL.
Use one page per call if a batch arrives damaged.
An unread, blocked, or unverified page cannot support a claim.
{% endif %}

{% if has('cite_documents') %}
The citations argument is a list of document citation objects.
Each object requires collection and file_hash. quote holds 5 to 6 consecutive words of exact source wording.
Repeat collection and file_hash in every object, including separate quotes from the same document.
Objects do not inherit arguments from earlier objects.
find optionally selects an exact phrase within quote. Omission uses the whole quote.
term optionally records the originating search query. why states the supported claim.
The result gives a handle for each verified entry. Failed entries give their reason and can give a candidate passage.
Retry only failed entries with exact wording. A verified quote does not by itself verify the writer or date of that quote.
```json
{"citations":[{"collection":"alpha","file_hash":"COPY_RETURNED_HASH","quote":"COPY_5_TO_6_SOURCE_WORDS"}]}
```
```json
{"citations":[{"collection":"alpha","file_hash":"COPY_RETURNED_HASH","quote":"COPY_5_TO_6_SOURCE_WORDS","find":"COPY_EXACT_PHRASE_INSIDE_QUOTE","term":"original search","why":"This passage supports the stated approval."}]}
```
This batch verifies two passages from the same document.
```json
{"citations":[{"collection":"alpha","file_hash":"COPY_RETURNED_HASH","quote":"COPY_FIRST_5_TO_6_SOURCE_WORDS"},{"collection":"alpha","file_hash":"COPY_RETURNED_HASH","quote":"COPY_SECOND_5_TO_6_SOURCE_WORDS"}]}
```
{% endif %}
